  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable temperatures and dry through Saturday.\n\nAn upper level trough is located over the Northeast and \nAtlantic Canada early this morning. At the surface, ~1024mb high\npressure is centered over the eastern Great Lakes, and is \nbuilding SE toward the Mid-Atlantic in the wake of a dry cold \nfront sliding N-S along the coast. Clear early this morning \naside from some patchy mid level clouds in vicinity of the VA/NC\nborder. Temperatures have cooled into the upper 40s to lower \n50s across the northern tier of the area, and have actually \nrisen into the upper 50s/lower 60s over southern VA/NE NC due to\nmixing with the frontal passage. Overall, temperatures in this \narea should cool several degrees through sunrise in the wake of \nthe front while dropping a few degrees into the mid to upper 40s\nacross the northern tier of the area.\n\nHigh temperatures today will be slightly below average and \ngenerally ranging from the the mid 60s N to lower 70s S despite \nfull (or nearly full) sunshine. Tonight will be the coolest of \nthe week (and of the season so far) with widespread lows in the \nlower to mid 40s, with 50s at the immediate coast as high \npressure settles over the region. Some upper 30s are possible in\nrural and typically cooler locations in the Piedmont. High \npressure shifts offshore Wednesday with high temperatures \nmoderating back to near average, which are in the lower to mid \n70s. The high shifts farther offshore Thursday with high \ntemperatures rising into the upper 70s to lower 80s (potentially\nmid 80s well inland) ahead of another dry cold front. This cold\nfront crosses the region Thursday night with temperatures \ntrending down to near seasonal averages Friday and Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Moisture potentially returns late this weekend \ninto early next week.\n\nBy late this weekend into early next week, there is a potential\nthat moisture gets pulled northward from the western Gulf \n(associated with a probable tropical cyclone) and then pushed \neastward across the region ahead of a trough over the mid-\nMississippi Valley. Confidence remains low with regard to \ndetails as a tropical depression has yet to form. However, most \nglobal models and ensemble systems depict the TC remnants \nmerging with the upper trough in vicinity of the Mid-South with \naccumulating QPF over the local area later Sunday/Monday \ntimeframe. As would be expected, there is considerable spread \nbetween the 10th and 90th percentile QPF in the 00z/06 ensemble \nguidance (EPS/GEFS/GEPS). Temperatures should remain near \naverage late this weekend and early next week.",
